     "body": "**MCP (Model Context Protocol)**: an open protocol that provides tools, data and prompts to AI apps **in a standard form**. There are servers (providers) and clients (Claude Code, Claude Desktop, your own app).\n\n- **What can be provided**: **tools** (operations the model calls), **resources** (readable content: files, records, catalogs), **prompts** (canned templates)\n- **Transports**: **stdio** (started locally as a child process; for development and personal use) / **HTTP (Streamable HTTP; formerly SSE)** (remote; for teams and production; needs authentication such as OAuth)\n- **Building**: with the official SDKs (Python, TypeScript and others), define tools as functions and attach a description and schema. **The quality of the tool description decides how often it is used**\n- **Deploying**: stdio if local, remote HTTP if shared. Secrets in environment variables\n- **Integrating**: in Claude Code, register in `.mcp.json` (shared with the project) or user settings. From the Messages API, the MCP connector links a remote server directly\n- Do not pack too many tools into one server. It eats context and causes wrong selection\n\n### 🎓 Points to remember\n- **Three things a server exposes**: **tools** (operations the model calls) / **resources** (read-only data the client fetches and puts into context; some at fixed addresses, some **templates that take arguments**; for data known from the start of the turn) / **prompts** (canned instructions invoked by name; when you want crafted wording to have the same quality on every client)\n- **Transports**: **stdio** (a local child process; your own machine only; cannot be used for sharing or remote) / **HTTP** (over the network; recommended for remote and shared) / **SSE** (the older method from before HTTP; **not used for new work**)\n- **Configuration scope (who loads it)**: **local** (the per-project entry in `~/.claude.json`; you, in that project only) / **user** (all your projects) / **project** (`.mcp.json` at the repository root; everyone who clones; **a stdio server is started on each person's machine, so it needs a runtime there**) / **enterprise** (distributed by admins; cannot be overridden). A shared team server is **HTTP plus project or enterprise**. Writing stdio in `.mcp.json` shares less than it appears to\n- **Permissions per MCP tool**: permission rules name individual tools as `mcp__server-name__tool-name` (deny only the write tools, for example). In the API's MCP connector, `enabled` on `mcp_toolset` narrows which tools the model sees at all, and `defer_loading` holds back definitions until needed (needs a beta header at the time of writing; only servers exposed over HTTP are supported). **A permission rule is “may it run” (control); enabled is “is it visible at all” (context cost)**\n- Claude Code defers loading tool definitions by default and reads only what a search turns up. Even so, narrow the servers you connect\n- **Authentication examples**: the GitHub MCP server (remote HTTP; a personal access token as Bearer; the token lives in an environment variable and config refers to it by variable name) / the Linear MCP server (OAuth; sign in in the browser the first time; the token is saved automatically; an authorization model tied to the user's identity)\n\n> “We want to use an internal database from both Claude Code and our own app” → make it an MCP server (build once, use from several clients). “Share with the team” → remote HTTP plus authentication.",
